## Summary

`scene.save('x.obj')` produces no output file, and no error, when the mesh is attached with `rootNode.createChildNode(name, mesh)`. The same mesh attached directly to `rootNode.entity` is exported.

## Environment

- Revision `66cb26df3b031f0bf6976d4e88dc89721983afa4` (default branch `master`)
- Node.js v22.23.3 and npm 10.9.9 (Docker image `node:22-slim`, Linux); the same results on Windows with Node.js v22.11.0
- `npm install` and `npm run build` complete without errors; verified on 2026-10-16

## Steps to reproduce

```sh
npm install
npm run build
node repro.js   # repro.js holds the script below, saved in the repository root
```

```js
const fs = require('fs');
const { Scene } = require('./dist/aspose/threed');
const { Mesh } = require('./dist/aspose/threed/entities');
const { Vector4 } = require('./dist/aspose/threed/utilities');

function triangle() {
  const mesh = new Mesh('tri');
  mesh.controlPoints = [new Vector4(10, 20, 30, 1), new Vector4(11, 20, 30, 1), new Vector4(10, 21, 30, 1)];
  mesh.createPolygon(0, 1, 2);
  return mesh;
}

const root = new Scene();
root.rootNode.entity = triangle();
root.save('root.obj');
console.log('root.obj written:', fs.existsSync('root.obj'));

const child = new Scene();
child.rootNode.createChildNode('n', triangle());
child.save('child.obj');
console.log('child.obj written:', fs.existsSync('child.obj'));
```

## Expected

`child.obj` is written with the `v` and `f` lines of the triangle, as `root.obj` is (or an error says why not).

## Actual result

```
root.obj written: true
child.obj written: false
```
No exception is thrown.

## Where in the repository

- `src/aspose/threed/formats/obj/ObjExporter.ts:62-76`: `_collectNodes` recurses into `node.childNodes` only inside `if (mesh !== undefined && ...)` (`:64`), so a root node without an entity ends the walk before its children are visited
- `src/aspose/threed/Scene.ts:331`: the file stream writes the file on `close()` only when its content is non-empty, so an empty export leaves no file

